{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "lem:canonical-wellposedness",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every finite deterministic collection \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in\\mathbb R\\), the variables \\(D(x_1),\\ldots,D(x_m)\\) are finite and nonnegative almost surely. Moreover, for every \\(r\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\n (D(x_1+r),\\ldots,D(x_m+r))\\stackrel{d}{=}(D(x_1),\\ldots,D(x_m)).\n\\]\nNo separable locally bounded version, continuum contact-radius bound, or block-independence conclusion is asserted.",
    "depends_on": [
      "def:canonical-field",
      "def:excursion-regime",
      "lem:banerjee-wellner-fixed-location-lr"
    ],
    "justification": "Places the canonical field on the firm fixed-location footing actually supported by the source and Brownian equivariance.",
    "gap": "No separable locally bounded version or continuum contact-radius tail has been constructed.",
    "consumer": "Finite-dimensional stationarity is valid; continuum maxima and block separation remain open.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix a finite deterministic collection \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\in\\mathbb R\\).  For \\(x\\in\\mathbb R\\), define the recentered increment path\n\\[\n \\mathbb W_x(t)=\\mathbb W(x+t)-\\mathbb W(x),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n\\]\nThe definition of \\(X\\) in \\(\\texttt{def:canonical-field}\\) gives the pathwise identity\n\\[\n X(x+t)-X(x)-2xt=\\mathbb W_x(t)+t^2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nWe record the GCM equivariance used with (1).  If \\(G\\) is the greatest convex minorant of a function \\(F\\), then\n\\[\n \\widetilde G(t)=G(x+t)-G(x)-2xt\n\\]\nis the greatest convex minorant of\n\\(\n \\widetilde F(t)=F(x+t)-F(x)-2xt\n\\).\nIndeed, \\(\\widetilde G\\) is convex and lies below \\(\\widetilde F\\).  Conversely, translating and adding back the same affine function maps every convex minorant of \\(\\widetilde F\\) to a convex minorant of \\(F\\), which must lie below \\(G\\).  The identical order argument on the two half-lines proves equivariance of the constrained one-sided minorants.  Under this transformation the slope constraint \\(2x\\) becomes the origin constraint \\(0\\), and both unrestricted and constrained slopes are reduced by \\(2x\\).  Changing variables in the energy integral of \\(\\texttt{def:canonical-field}\\) therefore yields, on every path on which the fixed-location energies are defined,\n\\[\n D(x)=\\Phi(\\mathbb W_x). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBy the independent stationary Gaussian increment property of \\(\\mathbb W\\) in \\(\\texttt{def:canonical-field}\\), \\(\\mathbb W_x\\) has the law of a two-sided standard Brownian motion.  Since \\(D(0)=\\Phi(\\mathbb W)\\), (2) implies\n\\[\n D(x)\\stackrel{d}{=}D(0). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe cited fixed-location result \\(\\texttt{lem:banerjee-wellner-fixed-location-lr}\\) says that \\(D(0)\\) is a finite nonnegative random variable almost surely.  Equation (3) gives the same conclusion for each deterministic \\(x_j\\).  Because the collection is finite, intersecting the corresponding probability-one events proves simultaneous finiteness and nonnegativity of \\(D(x_1),\\ldots,D(x_m)\\).\n\nIt remains to prove the joint, rather than merely marginal, stationarity assertion.  Fix \\(r\\in\\mathbb R\\).  For arbitrary indices \\(i,j\\) and times \\(s,t\\), the covariance\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\mathbb W_{x_i+r}(s),\\mathbb W_{x_j+r}(t)\\right)\n\\]\nis the signed overlap of the two Brownian increment intervals with endpoints \\((x_i+r,x_i+r+s)\\) and \\((x_j+r,x_j+r+t)\\).  Translating all four endpoints by \\(-r\\) leaves that signed overlap unchanged.  Thus the centered Gaussian vector process\n\\[\n \\bigl(\\mathbb W_{x_1+r},\\ldots,\\mathbb W_{x_m+r}\\bigr)\n\\]\nhas the same finite-dimensional distributions as\n\\(\n \\bigl(\\mathbb W_{x_1},\\ldots,\\mathbb W_{x_m}\\bigr)\n\\).\nApplying the measurable fixed-location functional \\(\\Phi\\) coordinatewise in (2) gives\n\\[\n (D(x_1+r),\\ldots,D(x_m+r))\n \\stackrel{d}{=}(D(x_1),\\ldots,D(x_m)). \\tag{4}\n\\]\nOnly finitely many fixed-location functionals enter this argument.  Consequently, (1)--(4) prove no separability, local boundedness, continuum contact-radius control, or block independence, in agreement with the stated scope.",
